1: \subsection{Corrected physical reference returns and their actual fan comparisons}
2: \begin{statement}{P3K.69}{Proved}
3: Use the physical line \(M\), its meridian-one Hermitian dual
4: \(U=M^{-1}\), and the complete circle, cap and sphere-gluing arrows
5: of P3K.68. Write
6: \[
7:  A_M=(4k_M-\ell_M)/24,\qquad A_U=(4k_U-\ell_U)/24.
8: \]
9: The actual oriented sphere return is \(A_M=-A_U\) modulo 28.
10: The following are corrected consequences for the already constructed
11: families and their specified positive parameter loops.
12: 
13: \begin{enumerate}
14: \item For the canonical whole-zero reference pages of K26, K28 and
15: K32, with \(H=Ma^2/d\) and page value \(\chi\), the actual physical
16: integer is \(H/2-\chi\). The retained characteristic and native free
17: coordinates are
18: \[
19:  (k_U,\ell_U)=(-3H,-24\chi),\quad
20:  (k_M,\ell_M)=(3H,24\chi),\quad
21:  (m,n)=(6\chi,3H-6\chi).
22: \]
23: The primitive K22 groups with \(d=8,14,20,30\) have physical integers
24: \(3,6,9,14\). The explicit primitive OS45 family of K10 has class
25: \(3\); its actual \(T_2\) fan of K64 has class \(2\).
26: The weighted degree-four OS47 example of F25 has class \(4\).
27: The reduced III families of K24 have physical integer \(3l+2\).
28: \item An actual fixed-edge flop in K23 has physical class
29: \(7A_M\). Its four primitive reference examples therefore have
30: flopped classes \(21,14,7,14\), respectively. The weighted OS47
31: example has fixed-edge-flopped class zero.
32: \item The selected-cusp source loop of F56 has initial and reflected
33: physical class \(-1=27\). Its initial native fixed-star coordinates
34: are \((0,-6,-1)\), and its ordinary coordinates there are
35: \((6,-6,-1)\). The first flops at \(C_0,C_1,C_2\) have classes
36: \(21,21,27\). On every actual physical-vertical-edge fan
37: \(T_n=T(e_1+ne_2,e_2)\), its physical class is
38: \[
39:                   s_n=-1-3n(n-1)\pmod{28},
40: \]
41: with exact image \(\{5,7,9,13,19,21,23,27\}\).
42: \item On the actual two mutation families of K59 the physical classes
43: are
44: \[
45:  [\Phi_{P_j}]=-1-3j(j+1),\qquad
46:  [\Phi_{R_j}]=-7-24j(j+1)\pmod{28}\quad(j\geq0).
47: \]
48: All its variation matrices, stable derivatives and native free pairs
49: are retained. The second sequence has least positive period seven
50: and exact image \(\{1,13,17,21\}\).
51: \item In the two initial/reflected E7 source objects of K57--60, put
52: \(A_{\rm scalar}=0\) for \((a,b,m)=(1,2,4p)\), and
53: \(A_{\rm scalar}=3p-1\) for \((a,b,m)=(2,1,4p-1)\).
54: The fixed-scalar infinity physical returns are respectively \(0,-1\).
55: For the literal global scalar \(\lambda=v^{km}\lambda_0\),
56: \[
57: \begin{array}{c|cc}
58:  (a,b,m)&I_T&Z_T+Z_{JT}\\\hline
59:  (1,2,4p)&0&1\\
60:  (2,1,4p-1)&-1-2k(3p-1)&2+2k(3p-1).
61: \end{array}
62: \]
63: Together with the actual single-loop difference \(Z_{JT}-Z_T=a\),
64: this leaves exactly the two possibilities
65: \[
66:  (Z_T,Z_{JT})=(kA_{\rm scalar}+14e,
67:                  a+kA_{\rm scalar}+14e),\qquad e\in\{0,1\}.
68: \]
69: No value of \(e\) is assigned. The smallest good case has pairs
70: \((0,1)\) or \((14,15)\); the reduced case \(p=k=1\) has pairs
71: \((2,4)\) or \((16,18)\).
72: \end{enumerate}
73: The reference zeros of the original family and the positive OS49
74: fan remain zero. The actual OS49 negative-fan classes \(-3k\) and
75: \(-k\) in K65 are unchanged, since their directed flop comparison
76: starts from that zero reference.
77: \end{statement}
78: \begin{proof}
79: \paragraph{The exact line map and the scope of the correction.}
80: K68 constructs the Hermitian functional from each nonzero physical
81: vector, on both the ordinary source and each normal cap. It reverses
82: the circle character and gives \(c_1(U)=-c_1(M)\). Its local F9
83: coordinate calculation gives meridian value one for \(U\).
84: On the regular page the restriction of the K6 Thom class is therefore
85: \(-c_1(M)\). In K7 the graph-normal Euler class restricts as
86: \(-2c_1(M)\), so its square is exactly the previously computed
87: \(4c_1(M)^2\). No source, divisor, graph or cap is replaced.
88: The original real trace computes \(A_U\).
89: 
90: The physical-to-dual comparison in K68 retains the oriented quotient
91: and changes its two-sphere marking by the actual antipodal map. On
92: both Hopf-gluing pieces it is the identity on the quaternionic factor
93: and the reflection on the other four-ball. The map has degree minus
94: one in dimension eight. In physical positive-line coordinates the
95: pieces are \(-R,-L\), whereas in dual positive-line coordinates they
96: are \(R,L\), where
97: \(L=D^4_y\times S^3_q\) and \(R=S^3_y\times D^4_q\).
98: The first-to-second identification becomes \(F^{-1}\) when these
99: pieces are put back in \(L,R\) order. Consequently the exact arrows
100: are
101: \[
102:  \lambda_M(F)=-\Sigma_{F^{-1}}=\Sigma_F,\qquad
103:  \lambda_U(F)=\Sigma_{F^{-1}}=-\Sigma_F.
104: \]
105: The inverse equality follows by the explicit opposite-collar
106: cancellation after removing the extending \(L,R\) generators.
107: This accounts for the orientation and the inverse separately.
108: Applying F22 to its specified positive Chern line gives
109: \(\mu(\Sigma_F)=A_M/28=-A_U/28\).
110: 
111: \paragraph{Reference pages and a fixed-edge comparison.}
112: The retained page proofs give
113: \(k_U=-3H\), \(\ell_U=-24\chi\). Duality changes each odd
114: characteristic pairing by minus one, yielding the two pairs in the
115: statement and \(A_M=H/2-\chi\). The actual K27/K30 Cox framing
116: comparison is unchanged: its relative integers were computed from
117: these same \(U\)-pairings by
118: \[
119:  k_U=-m-n,\qquad \ell_U=-4m.
120: \]
121: Equivalently \(k_M=m+n\), \(\ell_M=4m\). Solving the two displayed
122: integral equalities gives \(m=6\chi\), \(n=3H-6\chi=6A_M\).
123: This proves the claimed characteristic and relative dictionary without
124: altering a native product.
125: 
126: For a primitive rational source \(H=d\) and \(\chi=1\), giving
127: \(d/2-1\). K10 supplies the complete OS45 equation, section,
128: linearization zero and ordinary cap, and K27.2 fixes its primitive
129: scalar winding as one. K64's actual directed \(C_2\) comparison at
130: \(T_1\) therefore subtracts one from its reference value three;
131: its \(T_2\) value is two. For F25 the retained actual degree-four
132: page has \(H=10\), \(\chi=1\), giving four. For K24 use its literal previously proved dual index \(A_U=-(3l+2)\), obtained in
133: its full page computation. The exact dual map just proved gives
134: \(A_M=3l+2\). No root Euler number is inferred from a residue.
135: 
136: For a fixed curve, K23's full common-link map remains
137: \(T(y,q)=(q^{-1}yq,q)\). Its proved relative conjugation is
138: \[
139:  T(L^mR^nC^s)T^{-1}=L^{-n}R^{m+2n}C^{s+n}.
140: \]
141: Its central generator has \(\mu(C)=1/28\) by the oriented
142: three-ball plumbing. The physical initial central class is
143: \(s=A_M\), and the exact Gysin/derivative dictionary above gives
144: \(n=6A_M\). Thus the actual flopped class is \(7A_M\).
145: Substituting \(A_M=3,6,9,14,4\) gives the stated five examples.
146: 
147: \paragraph{The selected source loop and its vertical fans.}
148: K42/K68 retain the actual anti-linear Cartier-to-Cox normal map and
149: both cap products. They give \((k_U,\ell_U)=(6,0)\), hence
150: \((k_M,\ell_M)=(-6,0)\), \(A_M=-1\), and native free pair
151: \((0,-6)\). K50's explicit inverse cylinder still adds \(L^6\),
152: so its ordinary pair is \((6,-6)\). This operation extends over the
153: normal four-disc and introduces no central factor. K51 and K52's
154: literal orbit and stable-derivative prisms are also unchanged; the
155: three native pairs are \((0,-6),(0,-6),(-6,0)\).
156: Adding their respective right integers to the physical baseline
157: \(-1\) gives \((-7,-7,-1)=(21,21,27)\) modulo 28.
158: 
159: The K45 half-source arrow carries the full source, line and finite-cap
160: maps and preserves the physical orientation. Its reflected initial
161: return therefore has the same class \(-1\). K56's native Cox arrows
162: carry every bounded unit and preserve the actual height. On \(T_n\)
163: they have right integers \(6n-6\) and \(-6n\), and their respective
164: target fans are \(T_{n-1}\) and \(T_{n+1}\). Consequently
165: \[
166:  s_{n+1}-s_n=-6n,\qquad s_0=-1.
167: \]
168: The telescoping is an equality in the stated group for every signed
169: integer \(n\), and gives \(s_n=-1-3n(n-1)\).
170: The actual lattice arrows and enumeration in K56 still identify the
171: whole stated vertical-edge domain. Its difference under \(n\mapsto
172: n+28\) is \(28(-6n-81)\). Direct evaluation of the 28 residues,
173: executed in the certificate below, gives the displayed image.
174: 
175: \paragraph{The two mutation families.}
176: K59's actual source/period/normal maps give the unchanged directed
177: update
178: \[
179:  V'_{i'i'}=V_{ii},\qquad
180:  \delta'_{i'}=2V_{ii}-\delta_i,\qquad
181:  s'=s+(\delta_i-V_{ii})/2.
182: \]
183: Its variation and derivative update equations contain no central
184: coordinate. Their initial matrices are the independently computed
185: K51/K52 matrices, unchanged above. Induction through exactly its
186: specified sequence therefore retains every matrix and free pair.
187: Only the initial central value changes from the historical one to
188: \(-1\). Every later central value changes by the same minus two,
189: because each directed increment uses those retained matrices.
190: This gives the two formulas in the statement. For the second,
191: \(-7-24j(j+1)\equiv21+4j(j+1)\pmod{28}\). Its first seven
192: values are \(21,1,17,13,17,1,21\). The sequence has period seven
193: and is not constant; since seven is prime, this is its least positive
194: period. The range follows from those seven values.
195: 
196: \paragraph{The source-infinity comparison and paired source-zero returns.}
197: K68 checks the actual connection in K57, IE.4--5: it is normalized
198: on the positive physical circle, and its curvature represents
199: \(c_1(U)\). Its complete local residue and collar calculation
200: therefore gives \(A_U=0,1\). The physical infinity indices are
201: \(A_M=0,-1\). This uses the same source blowups, finite-root maps,
202: line clutches and analytic scalar bounds; no new infinity filling
203: is invoked.
204: 
205: K58's actual four-holed parameter surface and its cut maps give
206: \(Z_T+Z_{JT}+S+I_T=0\), where \(S\) is the selected-cusp return.
207: The actual selected loop identified in its RG.4 is the same loop as
208: F56, so the corrected value is \(S=-1\). Its scalar winding at
209: infinity is exactly \(-2k\) in the independent root scalar.
210: The same pair-of-pants morphism consequently gives
211: \(I_T=I_{\rm fixed}-2kA_{\rm scalar}\).
212: The good reference scalar is zero; the reduced reference scalar is
213: \(3p-1\), by the reference calculation above. Substitution yields
214: the table and the common paired sum
215: \[
216:                     Z_T+Z_{JT}=a+2kA_{\rm scalar}.
217: \]
218: K60's independently constructed single native product and its literal
219: double pullback give \((m,n)=(1-a,a)\). Its directed Cox map thus
220: still gives \(Z_{JT}-Z_T=a\). Subtracting this equality from the
221: paired sum gives
222: \(2(Z_T-kA_{\rm scalar})=0\) in \(\mathbb Z/28\).
223: The kernel of multiplication by two is exactly \(\{0,14\}\), so
224: the displayed two ordered possibilities follow. Both satisfy both
225: integral congruences, and their remaining order-two choice has not
226: been supplied by any of these maps.
227: 
228: Finally, multiplication of either zero reference integer by minus
229: one gives zero. K65 starts its OS49 comparison from that independently
230: constructed positive-fan zero, and uses the actual negative-to-positive
231: native right exponent, which is unchanged by the line dictionary.
232: Its negative-fan classes \(-3k\) and \(-k\) are therefore retained.
233: The exact arithmetic certificate is
234: \path{code/part3_root/physical_return_consequences.py}.
235: \end{proof}
